ightmargin=0.5cm,\n  framexleftmargin=3mm,\n  framexrightmargin=3mm,\n  framesep=2mm,\n  rulecolor=\\color{subheadingcolor}\n}\n\n% Tables with text wrapping and proper column widths\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{longtable}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{calc}\n\n% Increase row height for readability\n\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.4}\n\n% Allow text wrapping in table cells by default\n\\newcolumntype{L}[1]{>{\\raggedright\\arraybackslash}p{#1}}\n\\newcolumntype{C}[1]{>{\\centering\\arraybackslash}p{#1}}\n\\newcolumntype{R}[1]{>{\\raggedleft\\arraybackslash}p{#1}}\n\n% Force Pandoc longtables to use full width and wrap text\n\\usepackage{etoolbox}\n\\AtBeginEnvironment{longtable}{\\footnotesize}\n\\makeatletter\n\\let\\oldlt\\longtable\n\\let\\endoldlt\\endlongtable\n\\def\\longtable{\\@ifnextchar[\\longtable@i \\longtable@ii}\n\\def\\longtable@i[#1]{\\begin{oldlt}[#1]}\n\\def\\longtable@ii{\\begin{oldlt}[c]}\n\\def\\endlongtable{\\end{oldlt}}\n\\makeatother\n\n% Prevent table columns from overflowing page margins\n\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n\n% Links\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\hypersetup{\n  colorlinks=true,\n  linkcolor=headingcolor,\n  urlcolor=linkblue,\n  pdfborder={0 0 0}\n}\n\n% Page header/footer\n\\usepackage{fancyhdr}\n\\pagestyle{fancy}\n\\fancyhf{}\n\\renewcommand{\\headrulewidth}{0.5pt}\n\\renewcommand{\\footrulewidth}{0.5pt}\n\\fancyhead[L]{\\leftmark}\n\\fancyhead[R]{\\thepage}\n\\fancyfoot[C]{\\small \\textbf{AI-generated} Study Material | \\textbf{Ram Kumar}, saas.expert.ram@gmail.com}\n\n% Better handling of images\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\setkeys{Gin}{width=0.8\\textwidth}\n\n% Line spacing\n\\usepackage{setspace}\n\\setstretch{1.15}\n\\usepackage{bookmark}\n\\IfFileExists{xurl.sty}{\\usepackage{xurl}}{} % add URL line breaks if available\n\\urlstyle{same}\n\\hypersetup{\n  hidelinks,\n  pdfcreator={LaTeX via pandoc}}\n\n\\author{}\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\n{\n\\setcounter{tocdepth}{3}\n\\tableofcontents\n}\n\\section{Cloud Security Operations: EC2 Threat Detection, Incident\nClassification, and Response\nStrategies}\\label{cloud-security-operations-ec2-threat-detection-incident-classification-and-response-strategies}\n\n\\subsection{1. Learning Objectives}\\label{learning-objectives}\n\nAfter completing this chapter, the reader will be able to:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\def\\labelenumi{\\arabic{enumi}.}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Classify Cloud Security Findings:} Accurately identify and\n  differentiate between various threat indicators, including credential\n  compromise, malware injection, and reconnaissance activities.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Implement Credential Hygiene:} Apply rigorous access control\n  policies to prevent Root and Administrator credential misuse on\n  virtualized infrastructure.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Detect Network Anomalies:} Recognize patterns indicative of\n  port scanning, denial-of-service (DoS) attacks, and unauthorized\n  outbound traffic (e.g., Tor usage).\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Develop Incident Response Plans:} Formulate actionable\n  workflows for containing EC2 compromise and restoring service\n  integrity.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\subsection{2. Executive Overview}\\label{executive-overview}\n\nIn the modern cloud computing landscape, virtualized computing\nenvironments operate as distributed systems where the physical boundary\nof an organization’s perimeter is often replaced by logical security\ncontrols. Within this architecture, Amazon Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2)\ninstances serve as the primary execution point for workloads, data\nprocessing, and application hosting. Consequently, the security posture\nof these instances is critical; a single compromised instance can serve\nas a pivot point for lateral movement across a wider network\ninfrastructure.\n\nThe management of security in this environment relies heavily on\nmonitoring, detection, and response mechanisms. Security vendors and\ncloud-native tools monitor EC2 instances for specific indicators of\ncompromise (IoCs). These indicators manifest as security findings that\nrequire immediate attention. Understanding the taxonomy of these\nthreats—from simple reconnaissance to active malware deployment—is\nessential for maintaining a secure cloud posture.\n\nThis domain addresses the problem of maintaining confidentiality,\nintegrity, and availability (CIA) within virtualized environments. It\nsolves the problem of how organizations respond to automated alerts\ngenerated by cloud security posture management (CSPM) tools.\nFurthermore, it situates EC2 security within the broader context of\nidentity and access management (IAM), network segmentation, and incident\nresponse frameworks defined by standards such as NIST CSF (Cybersecurity\nFramework).\n\n\\subsection{3. Core Concepts \\& Technical\nDefinitions}\\label{core-concepts-technical-definitions}\n\n\\textbf{Security Finding:} A notification or record generated by a\nsecurity management system that indicates a potential security issue,\nvulnerability, or policy violation. These findings typically categorize\nthe severity, urgency, and resource type (e.g., \\texttt{EC2},\n\\texttt{IAM\\ User}) associated with the threat.\n\n\\textbf{Root Credential:} The administrative keys and passwords (access\nkeys) associated with the root account of a cloud environment. These\ncredentials grant unrestricted access to all resources. Unlike IAM\nusers, these are often not managed by role-based access control (RBAC)\nand pose a critical risk if exposed.\n\n\\textbf{Reconnaissance:} The act of gathering information about a target\nsystem or network, often performed by an attacker before initiating an\nexploit. In cloud monitoring, this includes detecting automated network\nsweeps or information disclosure attempts.\n\n\\textbf{Denial-of-Service (DoS):} An attack where the availability of a\nservice is compromised by overwhelming a system with traffic or\nexploiting vulnerabilities to crash the service. A DoS vector in the\ncloud often targets the compute capacity or the underlying networking\nlayer.\n\n\\textbf{Portscan:} A network operation used to determine which TCP or\nUDP ports are open or closed on a target host. In a security context,\nthis is an indicator of an attacker mapping the network topology to\nidentify vulnerable services.\n\n\\textbf{Tor (The Onion Router):} A system designed to allow internet\nusers to anonymously route their internet traffic through a volunteer\nnetwork of relays. While used for privacy by some, attackers utilize Tor\nto bypass geo-restrictions or hide their identity, making\n\\texttt{UnauthorizedAccess} events via Tor particularly difficult to\ntrace.\n\n\\textbf{Backdoor:} A method of bypassing normal authentication in a\ncomputer system or web server, often installed by an attacker to\nmaintain access even after the initial exploit has been closed. Unlike\nTrojans which are often payloads, backdoors focus on persistent access.\n\n\\subsection{4. In-Depth Subject Matter\nCoverage}\\label{in-depth-subject-matter-coverage}\n\n\\subsubsection{Identity and Access Control (Root and Admin\nUsage)}\\label{identity-and-access-control-root-and-admin-usage}\n\nThe management of identity is the first line of defense in cloud\nsecurity. Misconfiguration often leads to \\texttt{RootCredentialUsage}\nfindings. The root account should be treated as a “kill switch”—useful\nfor emergency recovery but dangerous if compromised permanently.\n\n\\textbf{Defining the Risk} Root credentials provide unrestricted access\nto billing information, key management, and all global services. If\nleaked, an attacker can modify IAM policies to lock out legitimate\nadministrators.\n\n\\textbf{Implementation Guidelines}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\def\\labelenumi{\\arabic{enumi}.}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Disable Root Access:} Configure the cloud environment to\n  require multi-factor authentication (MFA) for any root console sign-in\n  attempts.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Use IAM Roles:} Replace long-term access keys for applications\n  with AWS IAM Roles, which are temporary and scoped to specific\n  resources.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Rotate Credentials:} Regularly rotate root access keys and\n  immediately invalidate them if compromised.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\textbf{Best Practices} \\textgreater{} \\emph{Always assume the root\naccount is a high-value target. Do not use root access keys for CI/CD\npipelines or script execution. Instead, create an IAM user with specific\npermissions and attach a policy that allows only the necessary actions.}\n\n\\subsubsection{Network Reconnaissance and\nMapping}\\label{network-reconnaissance-and-mapping}\n\nAttackers frequently begin by mapping the attack surface. This involves\nscanning for open ports, running versions of services, and identifying\nsecurity groups that allow public ingress.\n\n\\textbf{Understanding the Indicators} A \\texttt{Recon:EC2/Portscan}\nfinding indicates that an entity is attempting to discover open services\non an instance. This is often a precursor to exploitation.\n\n\\textbf{Detection Strategies} Network monitoring tools utilize flow logs\n(VPC Flow Logs) and intrusion detection systems (IDS) to identify\nunusual outbound connection attempts. Legitimate traffic usually follows\na known pattern, while scans occur in short bursts across many ports.\n\n\\textbf{Mitigation Steps}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\def\\labelenumi{\\arabic{enumi}.}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Ingress Filtering:} Implement Network ACLs (NACLs) to restrict\n  traffic to only necessary ports (e.g., 80/443).\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Security Groups:} Configure Security Groups to follow a\n  “default deny” policy where possible.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Monitoring:} Enable logging on security groups and review\n  \\texttt{Security\\ Hub} findings for repeated connection attempts from\n  the same IP range.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\textbf{Common Pitfalls} \\textgreater{} \\emph{Blocking all inbound\ntraffic on a production instance often leads to service outage. Instead\nof blocking ports, use a whitelist approach. However, do not trust\nuser-created security groups without review. A security group allowing\n\\texttt{0.0.0.0/0} on SSH (22) is a critical risk vector for brute force\nattacks.}\n\n\\subsubsection{Malware Classification and\nPayloads}\\label{malware-classification-and-payloads}\n\nMalware in a cloud environment can be detected through behavior analysis\nrather than signature-based detection alone. Two distinct categories are\nrelevant here: Trojan and Backdoor.\n\n\\textbf{Trojans} A Trojan is a program that appears benign but performs\nmalicious functions. In the context of\n\\texttt{Trojan:EC2/BlackholeTraffic}, the instance is acting as a node\nto forward traffic, often associated with botnets or anonymizer\nservices.\n\n\\textbf{Backdoors} A backdoor allows unauthorized access. The\ndistinction between a Trojan and a Backdoor is subtle but vital: a\nTrojan is often the initial payload, while a Backdoor is the mechanism\nfor persistence. \\texttt{Backdoor:EC2/DenialOfService} indicates the\ninstance is being controlled to launch DDoS attacks.\n\n\\textbf{Analysis Techniques} To identify these threats, analyze the\nsystem process list and network connections. Look for processes spawning\nwithout a clear parent process, or unexpected network connections to\nunknown destinations.\n\n\\textbf{Defense in Depth}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\def\\labelenumi{\\arabic{enumi}.}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Antivirus/Anti-Malware:} Deploy cloud-native agents (such as\n  Amazon GuardDuty) to monitor for malware behavior.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Patch Management:} Ensure the operating system and\n  applications are up to date to close vulnerabilities that Trojans\n  might exploit.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Network Isolation:} Isolate critical instances from public\n  networks.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\textbf{Common Pitfalls} \\textgreater{} \\emph{Relying solely on\nsignature-based detection is insufficient against fileless malware.\nBehavioral analysis, such as monitoring for unexpected outbound traffic,\nis often more effective in catching \\texttt{BlackholeTraffic}\nanomalies.}\n\n\\subsubsection{Denial-of-Service and Traffic\nAnomalies}\\label{denial-of-service-and-traffic-anomalies}\n\nAvailability is a pillar of cloud security. When an EC2 instance is\ncompromised to launch a DoS attack, the traffic often manifests as\n“BlackholeTraffic.”\n\n\\textbf{Attack Vectors}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{TCP Flood:} Overloading a port with SYN packets without\n  completing the handshake.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{UDP Flood:} Targeting services that do not support\n  connection-oriented traffic.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Botnet Abuse:} Compromised instances joining a botnet to\n  amplify attacks.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Response Mechanisms}\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\def\\labelenumi{\\arabic{enumi}.}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Auto-Scaling:} Configure auto-scaling groups to remove\n  unhealthy instances that are generating malicious traffic.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{DDoS Protection:} Utilize services like AWS Shield to absorb\n  and mitigate volumetric attacks.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Traffic Analysis:} Use VPC Flow Logs to identify high-volume\n  connections originating from your instances to external IPs.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\textbf{Best Practices} \\textgreater{} \\emph{Implement rate limiting at\nthe application level (e.g., using AWS WAF). Additionally, configure\nyour Security Groups to limit the number of open ports that can accept\nincoming connections, reducing the attack surface for flood attacks.}\n\n\\subsubsection{Incident Response\nLifecycle}\\label{incident-response-lifecycle}\n\nWhen a \\texttt{Security\\ Finding} is triggered, a structured response is\nrequired to minimize impact.\n\n\\textbf{Phase 1: Identification} Confirm the finding. Is the\n\\texttt{UnauthorizedAccess} attempt from a known bad IP, or is it\ninternal traffic? Verify if the traffic is \\texttt{Tor} related, which\noften indicates a compromised internal host trying to exfiltrate data.\n\n\\textbf{Phase 2: Containment} Immediately terminate the compromised\ninstance to stop the attack. Isolate the subnet via VPC peering\nrestrictions.\n\n\\textbf{Phase 3: Eradication} Rebuild the instance from a known clean\nimage. Do not simply “reset” the instance, as malware may persist in the\nunderlying storage volume.\n\n\\textbf{Phase 4: Recovery} Restore data from immutable backups that were\nnot accessed during the incident. Update security policies to prevent\nrecurrence.\n\n\\textbf{Phase 5: Lessons Learned} Document the incident and update\nrunbooks.\n\n\\begin{center}\\rule{0.5\\linewidth}{0.5pt}\\end{center}\n\n\\subsection{5. Summary \\& Actionable\nTakeaways}\\label{summary-actionable-takeaways}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Root Credentials:} Treat the root account as a last resort.\n  Disable default root access and enforce MFA for console access.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Reconnaissance:} Monitor for port scans. Use Network ACLs and\n  Security Groups to minimize open ports.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Malware:} Differentiate between Trojans (payloads) and\n  Backdoors (persistence). Use behavioral monitoring to catch anomalies.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{DoS:} Protect availability by using Shield, Auto-Scaling, and\n  rate limiting.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Response:} Follow a structured Incident Response (IR)\n  workflow. Terminate compromised instances and restore from clean\n  backups.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{6. Glossary of Terms}\\label{glossary-of-terms}\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Access Key ID:} A unique identifier paired with a secret\n  access key that is used to authenticate API calls. It is analogous to\n  a username. If compromised, the associated permissions are abused.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Amazon GuardDuty:} A security service provided by a cloud\n  vendor that monitors your AWS environment for malicious or\n  unauthorized behavior. It analyzes VPC Flow Logs, DNS logs, and\n  CloudTrail logs.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Amazon VPC Flow Logs:} Logs that capture information about the\n  IP traffic that was received by or was sent to network interfaces in a\n  specific Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC).\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Botnet:} A collection of internet-controlled computers that\n  are used to generate a cyberattack by an attacker without the users’\n  consent. Compromised EC2 instances are often rented for this purpose.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Cloud Security Posture Management (CSPM):} A category of cloud\n  security solutions designed to help organizations monitor and improve\n  their cloud security posture by automating checks against compliance\n  benchmarks and security configurations.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{IAM User:} A principal in Identity and Access Management that\n  represents a person, service, or application. These users can have\n  passwords or access keys and are used for authentication.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Intrusion Detection System (IDS):} A hardware or software\n  component that detects suspicious or unauthorized activities on a\n  network or host, often alerting security analysts.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Malware:} Software designed to damage, disrupt, or gain\n  unauthorized access to a computer system, which includes Trojans,\n  viruses, and ransomware.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Network ACL:} A virtual firewall for your VPC that controls\n  traffic flow into and out of your VPC. It is a stateless filter,\n  meaning it evaluates traffic regardless of direction.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Port:} A network communication endpoint that identifies a\n  specific process or service. Examples include Port 80 for HTTP and\n  Port 22 for SSH.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Reconnaissance:} The process of gathering intelligence about a\n  target system, typically involving port scanning and enumeration of\n  available services.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Security Finding:} A discrete record generated by a security\n  tool that documents a detected risk, such as an open security group or\n  a known vulnerability.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Security Group:} A virtual firewall associated with EC2\n  instances that controls inbound and outbound traffic. It is a stateful\n  filter.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Secret Access Key:} A string that grants full administrative\n  access when combined with the Access Key ID. This must be kept private\n  and never exposed in code.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{Tor (The Onion Router):} A network protocol and software suite\n  that enables anonymous communication by routing internet traffic\n  through a network of volunteer relays, often used by attackers to mask\n  their source IP.\n\\item\n  \\textbf{VPC Peering:} A networking feature that allows you to connect\n  two Amazon VPCs so that instances in either VPC can communicate using\n  private IP addresses as if they were in the same network.\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsection{7. Knowledge Assessment}\\label{knowledge-assessment}\n\n\\subsubsection{Part A: Recall \\& Comprehension (10\nMCQs)}\\label{part-a-recall-comprehension-10-mcqs}\n\n\\textbf{Q1:} Which credential type should be avoided for routine API\nautomation in favor of IAM Roles?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    User Password\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Root Access Keys\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    IAM Role ARN\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    MFA Token\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q2:} What is the primary purpose of a VPC Flow Log in a security\ncontext?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To store user emails\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To capture network traffic metadata\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To encrypt data in transit\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To manage billing\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q3:} Which term describes a hidden network entry point allowing\nattackers to bypass authentication?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Firewall\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Backdoor\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Load Balancer\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Bastion Host\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q4:} What is the most immediate action when an EC2 instance is\nconfirmed to be launching DDoS traffic?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Update the software\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Terminate the instance\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Change the password\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Request a ticket\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q5:} Which finding type indicates that an attacker is actively\nprobing network ports?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    UnauthorizedAccess:EC2/TorIPCaller\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Recon:EC2/Portscan\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Backdoor:EC2/DenialOfService\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    RootCredentialUsage\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q6:} What is the significance of Tor usage detected in an access\nlog?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    It indicates high bandwidth usage\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    It indicates anonymized access, potentially from a compromised host\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    It indicates a connection speed issue\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    It is a standard backup protocol\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q7:} Which component acts as a stateful firewall for traffic\nbetween EC2 instances?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Network ACL\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Security Group\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Route Table\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Gateway Load Balancer\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q8:} What defines a “Portscan” finding?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    A successful login attempt\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    A sequence of requests to different ports on a target\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    A backup of a database\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    A change of network IP\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q9:} Why is it recommended to disable the root account’s\npassword-based access?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To force users to use MFA\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Because root credentials grant unlimited privileges globally\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    To speed up login times\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Because it is illegal to use root\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\textbf{Q10:} Which service automatically protects against DDoS attacks\non EC2?\n\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\tightlist\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    AWS Shield\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{1}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    AWS Config\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{2}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    Amazon Inspector\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\item\n  \\begin{enumerate}\n  \\def\\labelenumi{\\Alph{enumi})}\n  \\setcounter{enumi}{3}\n  \\tightlist\n  \\item\n    AWS CloudTrail\n  \\end{enumerate}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\subsubsection{Part B: Application \\& Analysis (5 Short Answer\nQuestions)}\\label{part-b-application-analysis-5-short-answer-questions}\n\n\\textbf{Q11:} You receive a \\texttt{Recon:EC2/Portscan} finding\nassociated with an internal web server. The web server is intended for\nexternal access only. The findings show inbound connections on SSH (22).\nExplain why this is a risk and how to remediate it using the provided\ndomain knowledge. \\emph{Answer:} {[}Detailed explanation demonstrating\npractical application.{]}\n\n\\textbf{Q12:} Describe the operational difference between a Trojan and a\nBackdoor in the context of EC2 monitoring. Why is identifying a Backdoor\nmore critical for long-term security? \\emph{Answer:} {[}Detailed\nexplanation demonstrating practical application.{]}\n\n\\textbf{Q13:} A Security Finding indicates\n\\texttt{UnauthorizedAccess:EC2/TorIPCaller}. Analyze the implication of\nTor usage in this scenario and outline the steps to mitigate this\nspecific risk vector. \\emph{Answer:} {[}Detailed explanation\ndemonstrating practical application.{]}\n\n\\textbf{Q14:} Explain the relationship between VPC Flow Logs and the\ndetection of a Denial-of-Service attack. How does traffic analysis\nassist in differentiating between legitimate spikes and attacks?\n\\emph{Answer:} {[}Detailed explanation demonstrating practical\napplication.{]}\n\n\\textbf{Q15:} A user attempts to automate a script using \\texttt{root}\naccess keys. Identify the security flaw and describe the recommended\nbest practice for credential management in scripts. \\emph{Answer:}\n{[}Detailed explanation demonstrating practical application.{]}\n\n\\subsubsection{Part C: Synthesis \\& Evaluation (2 Critical Thinking\nChallenges)}\\label{part-c-synthesis-evaluation-2-critical-thinking-challenges}\n\n\\textbf{Q16:} Design a security policy for a VPC hosting sensitive\nfinancial data. The policy must address preventing unauthorized access,\nmanaging root credentials, and monitoring for reconnaissance.\n\\emph{Answer:} Guidelines for a high-quality response.\n\n\\textbf{Q17:} Evaluate the trade-offs of enabling VPC Flow Logs at the\nhighest verbosity level versus a lower verbosity level. How does the\nchoice impact storage costs and the ability to detect\n\\texttt{Recon:EC2/Portscan} findings? \\emph{Answer:} Guidelines for a\nhigh-quality response.\n\n\\end{document}\n"
